update lr_explanation_steps t set body=v.nb, label=case when v.nl='' then t.label else v.nl end from (values
('4d006c9f-2adb-4b45-ad0a-c61058ef5e47'::uuid,'e2632d05168ef65add8ae905acc978f3','Line up what the psychologists studied against what the author concludes.

The research is about one schedule. Children in nine-month schools forget a significant amount during summer breaks. That''s it. Nothing in the evidence is about the twelve-month schedule. Yet the author claims that schedule "will insure" students do not forget during their breaks.

Where does that guarantee come from? The author is assuming forgetting is a problem of the nine-month schedule only. But the twelve-month schedule still has breaks. Three of them, each a month long.

Given that kids forget over a long summer, how could we argue the twelve-month plan is no better? Maybe kids forget plenty in a month, too. If a month away wipes out most of what a summer away does, the twelve-month schedule could mean three rounds of forgetting instead of one.

So the gap is in the comparison. The evidence shows a bad result in one situation, says nothing about the other, and the author treats the other as free of it.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('730ec4b7-2e8c-497f-b24e-11ad4b771c51'::uuid,'0fc0d08152cad15a92f7806b20bb18e5','Look for an answer saying the argument assumes, without evidence, that forgetting during breaks happens only under the nine-month schedule. A "fails to consider" version would also work: the argument fails to consider that students might forget during month-long breaks too. Either way, the answer should point at the missing evidence about the twelve-month schedule.','Goal','Goal'),
('aac5aeae-90fe-4f43-975e-7342189649ab'::uuid,'d2efdc0e0e243e46187ca775840d0b4a','Watch the word "crime" change jobs between the evidence and the conclusion.

The evidence is about classification. Vagrancy laws turn "innocuous everyday occurrences" into crimes. Acts that were legal yesterday are illegal today.

The conclusion is about amount. Vagrancy laws "increase crime." The columnist sets this against the laws'' purpose of reducing "criminal activity," which makes it sound like people are now doing more harmful things.

Picture a park where twenty people nap on benches every week. A vagrancy law passes. The same twenty people nap on the same benches. On paper, the town now has twenty crimes a week that it didn''t have before. Has anyone''s behavior changed? No. The count went up because the label changed.

So the evidence supports only "more acts are now called crimes." The conclusion needs "people are committing more crime." The columnist slides from one claim to the other as if they were the same.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('e242526d-7b83-4e28-97dd-4583986280a8'::uuid,'704cb2b38b8e165d060e6de25fc7444a','Look for an answer saying the argument confuses relabeling acts as crimes with an actual increase in criminal activity. It might say the argument fails to distinguish the two, or that it uses "crime" in two different senses.','Goal','Goal'),
('be542e31-fdb8-4f27-9efd-47a6c021fbd1'::uuid,'a06f0987e29377977b61d5e19435ecad','The evidence is a conditional. Fresh okra is required for the best seafood gumbo:

best gumbo → fresh okra

Its contrapositive tells us what happens without fresh okra:

no fresh okra → no best gumbo

That''s what the evidence supports. Now read the conclusion closely. Without reliable fresh okra, customers could not count on good gumbo:

no fresh okra → no good gumbo

"Best" became "good." Those are different standards. A gumbo can be good without being the best. Without fresh okra, Chris might still serve a very good gumbo that just isn''t the best one possible.

So the critic takes a requirement for the top level of quality and treats it as a requirement for a merely good level. Given the evidence, how could we argue customers can still count on good gumbo? Say good gumbo doesn''t need fresh okra at all.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('bdf29502-171b-4a69-a682-dcb73b880d1c'::uuid,'235d486bb690f455d35341a4ec31d444','Start with the first sentence, because the whole argument rests on it: people "can either fight for anarchy or they can tolerate totalitarian government control."

Those are the two extremes. Anarchy means no government at all. Totalitarian control means government controls "all facets of human life." What about everything in between? A government that runs the courts and the roads but leaves your personal life alone is neither one.

Now look at the support. The evidence attacks only total control: it destroys the spirit and makes life miserable. Fine. Given that, how could we still argue against fighting for anarchy? Point to a third option. If limited government is available, rejecting totalitarian control doesn''t push us to anarchy. It could just as easily push us to the middle.

The argument works only if those two options are the only ones. That''s a false choice: two options presented as if nothing else exists.

Notice the flaw sits in a premise. We usually accept premises as true, but when an argument reaches its conclusion by knocking out one side of an either/or, the right answer can point out that the either/or was never complete.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('dbf4f7ad-6583-4e0a-a7da-bcc3bbe42768'::uuid,'b9813e0033fc0f564281be79b1c0a3b2','The evidence is about words. We call a web page a "site," and we talk about "visiting" it. Those words also describe land.

The conclusion is about law. Trespass law, which protects land, should be extended to protect property in cyberspace.

The bridge between them is an analogy: web sites resemble real estate, so they should be treated like real estate. But what resemblance has been shown? Only that we use some of the same words. That''s a similarity in vocabulary, not in anything that matters to the law.

Test it with another shared word. We "drive" traffic to a web page. Should traffic laws apply to web pages? Of course not. Shared vocabulary doesn''t show that two things are alike in the ways a law cares about, such as how they can be entered, damaged, or used without permission.

Given the evidence, how could we argue against the conclusion? Point out that a web site might share nothing with land except the words we use for it.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('3d7044df-b611-43ef-bc37-d1034be5d990'::uuid,'c8e909be97700ddd3541556b8ff79b32','Start with the curious fact. The Swahili and the Oromo both built tombs with large pillars and paneled facades, and no other people in contact with the Swahili built them.

The historian''s explanation: Oromo culture influenced Swahili culture. That''s a claim about direction. The style traveled from the Oromo to the Swahili.

What evidence points that way? None. The evidence shows the two cultures share the style. It dates the Swahili tombs to the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries, but gives no date at all for the Oromo ones.

Given that, how could we argue the Swahili were not influenced by the Oromo? Flip the arrow. Maybe the Swahili built these tombs first and the Oromo picked up the style from them. Everything in the evidence fits that story just as well. (The two cultures could even have arrived at the style independently.)

For the Oromo to have influenced the Swahili, the Oromo must have had the style first. The historian takes that for granted.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('85f2aa0c-b959-4739-b2cd-80f2eb60e896'::uuid,'bf573070a99270505867345cb28f4a1f','Look for an answer saying the historian assumes the Oromo built these tombs before the Swahili did. A "fails to consider" version works too: the argument fails to consider that the Swahili may have influenced the Oromo.','Goal','Goal'),
('492f384f-6a7a-4799-a551-2ca7ac5c336a'::uuid,'cee5e26ae533844193dffe6e0bd8376d','Sort the celestial objects in this galaxy into three groups. Stars generate light. Planets don''t; they only reflect it. And there are "many other celestial objects" that are neither.

The conclusion says some of those other objects generate light. Why? The evidence never says whether the other objects generate light. The author seems to reason: planets don''t generate light, these objects aren''t planets, so some of them do. Here is the shape:

planet → does not generate light
some objects are not planets
so, some of those objects generate light

That''s an illegal negation. The rule tells us what is true of something that is a planet. It tells us nothing about things that aren''t planets. The author is treating planets as if they were the only objects that don''t generate light, and nothing supports that.

Given the evidence, how could we argue the conclusion is false? Suppose every one of those other objects is dark, like a planet. Then there are many objects that are neither stars nor planets, and none of them generates light.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('c7d28d53-3548-4654-945d-960ddbd4e443'::uuid,'1ec64aaf4ba494f821f52d9ef8358e16','The stem says "fails to consider," so look for a possibility that would wreck the conclusion. The best one: objects other than planets might also fail to generate light. The answer might put it exactly that way: planets are not the only objects that do not generate light.','Goal','Goal'),
('3763d81a-64c3-4fb9-ae0a-a4bc3c7a30cc'::uuid,'0abbc95396f34a9f9f4a1f32eeab6e23','Among patients who received less than six weeks of PT, about 31% showed major improvement, whether they were treated by a general practitioner or by a specialist. Among patients who received PT for longer, about 50% showed major improvement, again regardless of whether they were treated by a general practitioner or by a specialist.','Evidence','Evidence'),
('cb8adb6d-d0e9-4940-b968-8cdcd4c6227b'::uuid,'451b16dc66ad940bf4561180bc5ec3e7','The conclusion is about one patient''s choice: picking a specialist or a general practitioner won''t change your odds of major improvement. The evidence is about whole groups: across all patients, the two kinds of practitioner get the same rates.

Can equal group rates hide real differences? Try numbers. Suppose there are two kinds of injury, sprains and fractures, and each practitioner sees as many of one as the other. General practitioners get major improvement in 50% of sprains and 12% of fractures. Specialists get 12% of sprains and 50% of fractures. Both average 31%, the same figure the records show for short treatment. The records look identical.

Now you have a fracture. Does your choice matter? Very much: 50% with a specialist, 12% with a general practitioner. The equal totals hid the fact that each practitioner is better at a different injury.

So given the evidence, how could we argue the choice does matter? Point out that the overall numbers could be an average of very different results for different injuries.','Evaluate','Evaluate'),
('db1f7ed4-5792-4923-90d8-a4c6f39f24ae'::uuid,'464f5a334e02cfdc7742389271abfffa','Look for an answer saying the argument overlooks that the two kinds of practitioner might do better with different kinds of injuries or patients. Then equal overall rates would not mean equal odds for any one patient.','Goal','Goal')
) v(id,ob,nb,ol,nl) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;